WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:01.221
Three,

00:00:01.221 --> 00:00:02.070
two,

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.100
one.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:08.861
Drop the beat.

00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:12.651
If I could describe the TED-Ed experience
in one word - exhilarating.

00:00:12.651 --> 00:00:13.960
Innovating.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.207
Curiosity.

00:00:15.207 --> 00:00:16.441
Contagious.

00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:18.491
Oh my gosh, worthwhile.

00:00:18.491 --> 00:00:19.730
Inspiring.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:20.450
Eye-opening.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.252
Oh, that's two words technically but 
one word if you put a hyphen, eye-opening.

00:00:24.252 --> 00:00:26.090
Students from around the world

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.701
come to TED headquarters

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:30.491
to share their ideas.

00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:32.351
Hello.

00:00:32.351 --> 00:00:35.781
This is the very first time that we have
done an event like this.

00:00:35.781 --> 00:00:40.031
Every speaker you'll see today is a member
of a TED-Ed Club, like many of you.

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:43.812
It's so cool coming here and meeting
people from all around the world.

00:00:43.812 --> 00:00:45.841
It's just a very fun work environment

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:48.472
and everyone's willing to talk to you
and work with you,

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:50.492
and just show you what they've been doing,

00:00:50.492 --> 00:00:52.123
so that's educational experience,

00:00:52.123 --> 00:00:54.201
but at the same time 
a very fun experience.

00:00:54.201 --> 00:00:56.602
And I think that's a really, 
really special thing

00:00:56.602 --> 00:00:59.054
to meet people who've had
the same experience as me

00:00:59.054 --> 00:01:02.253
and we can talk it out and form
this community, like a family.

00:01:02.253 --> 00:01:05.402
Think about what motivated you all
to start clubs in your schools.

00:01:05.402 --> 00:01:07.961
It's spectacular, it's awesome,

00:01:07.961 --> 00:01:12.112
it's all student-based, which makes us
feel like we belong in society

00:01:12.112 --> 00:01:14.133
while still standing out.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:16.973
It's great, I don't want to miss
a second of it.

00:01:16.973 --> 00:01:18.722
It's so much fun, it's so much fun.

00:01:18.722 --> 00:01:22.153
I've met people from like Jim,
who's so small,

00:01:22.153 --> 00:01:25.172
to people who are already in college,

00:01:25.172 --> 00:01:28.663
and we're having such a good time,
all of us together.

00:01:28.663 --> 00:01:31.923
Dream team, what a dream team!

00:01:31.923 --> 00:01:35.585
I've made friends already and I've been
here for four hours, or something.

00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:40.292
I feel like I've really 
made life-long friends.

00:01:40.292 --> 00:01:44.284
Definitely an opportunity that I was glad
that I took it.

00:01:44.284 --> 00:01:47.493
If you could improve anything
about your school, what would it be?

00:01:47.493 --> 00:01:52.443
I think that this experience to me
is helping me grow as a person

00:01:52.443 --> 00:01:57.883
because it's exposing me to many different
things that I didn't know about before.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.824
My mind just went poof
right when I saw it.

00:02:00.824 --> 00:02:03.894
I was like there's something 
for me to start.

00:02:03.894 --> 00:02:08.033
Thank you.

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:11.853
Honestly, it's just so rewarding to see
my idea come to life

00:02:11.853 --> 00:02:15.754
and see other people appreciate it
and feel a connection to it.

00:02:15.754 --> 00:02:16.864
It means a lot.

00:02:16.864 --> 00:02:20.214
All languages are created and molded
by the culture of their speakers.

00:02:20.214 --> 00:02:23.085
The languages we speak not only
help us express our thoughts,

00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:26.103
but actually shape the very thoughts
we wish to express.

00:02:26.103 --> 00:02:27.113
People have asked me,

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:30.565
"So you do this TED Talk, now what
do you do once you're done with this?"

00:02:30.565 --> 00:02:32.846
And so I really want to go 
somewhere with this.

00:02:32.846 --> 00:02:36.844
I don't want to just give the talk
and be done. I want to make a difference.

00:02:36.844 --> 00:02:38.734
One of the best 
and most effective ways

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:41.175
you can help special education departments

00:02:41.175 --> 00:02:44.466
is by creating or joining
a peer helper program.

00:02:44.466 --> 00:02:46.644
It makes me feel fulfilled to see

00:02:46.644 --> 00:02:49.904
my fellow students get excited 
about their education

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:53.395
and about their ideas that
they want to share with the world.

00:02:53.395 --> 00:02:56.925
It's wonderful because it gives hope
to me and to my peers

00:02:56.925 --> 00:02:59.645
that we will be good leaders
in the future.

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:02.371
We actually will be able to handle stuff
when we're older

00:03:02.371 --> 00:03:03.914
and take care of the planet.

00:03:03.914 --> 00:03:08.775
It's a beautiful thing. I'm very grateful
for TED and for my club

00:03:08.775 --> 00:03:11.466
for making me a better person.

00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:17.165
I think it's important that every student 
tries to experience this at least once.

00:03:17.165 --> 00:03:20.196
Just come, enjoy, and be a part of it.

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:26.476
It is one experience that will probably
get you to New York.

00:03:26.476 --> 00:03:30.646
Start a TED-Ed Club today.

